.TH TM 3
.SH NAME
tm \- seconds resolution time conversion support
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[CB]#include <tm.h>\fP
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I tm
library supports conversion between
string date specifications,
seconds resolution
\f[CB]time_t\fP
clock values and
\f[CB]Tm_t\fP.
\f[CB]Tm_t\fP
contains the elements of
\f[CB]struct tm\fP
along with these additions:
.TP
\f[CB]uint32_t tm_nsec\fP
The subsecond portion of the time in nanoseconds.
.TP
\f[CB]Tm_zone_t* tm_zone\fP
The time zone name.
.PP
\f[CB]localtime()\fP
and
\f[CB]gmtime()\fP
(see
.BR ctime (3))
are used to determine local time zone and savings time information.
.PP
\f[CB]time_t\fP
values are the number of seconds since the epoch,
.BR "Jan 1 00:00:00 UTC 1970" ,
with leap seconds omitted.
.PP
The global variable
\f[CB]int tm_info.flags\fP
contains flags that allow all programs using the library
to be controlled in a consistent manner.
\f[CB]tm_info.flags\fP
is initialized by the
\f[CB]tminit()\fP
routine described below, and may be explicitly reset after
\f[CB]tminit()\fP
is called.
The flags are:
.TP
\f[CB]TM_ADJUST\fP
Set by
\f[CB]tminit()\fP
if
\f[CB]localtime()\fP
and
\f[CB]gmtime()\fP
do not compensate for leap seconds.
.TP
\f[CB]TM_LEAP\fP
\f[CB]time_t\fP
values are interpreted as if they include leap seconds.
Set by
\f[CB]tminit()\fP
if the
\f[CB]leap\fP
option is set in the
\f[CB]TM_OPTIONS\fP
environment variable.
.TP
\f[CB]TM_UTC\fP
Times are relative to
.B UTC
(universal coordinated time, i.e.,
.BR GMT ).
Otherwise times are relative to the local time zone.
Set by
\f[CB]tminit()\fP
if the time zone name matches one of
\f[CB]tm_info.format[43]\fP
through
\f[CB]tm_info.format[46]\fP
described below.
If the time zone name is not determined by
\f[CB]localtime()\fP
then the environment variables
\f[CB]TZNAME\fP
(as described in BSD 4.3) and
\f[CB]TZ\fP
(as described in System V)
are checked, in order.
If this fails then the time zone name is constructed using
the local time zone offset.
.TP
\f[CB]TM_PEDANTIC\fP
Pedantic date parse.
.TP
\f[CB]TM_DATESTYLE\fP
\f[CB]date\fP(1)
style
.I mmddHHMMccyy
interpretation.
.TP
\f[CB]TM_SUBSECOND\fP
Append nanosecond
\f[CB].%N\fP
to
\f[CB]%S\fP.
.PP
The routines are:
.TP
\f[CB]time_t tmdate(const char* \fIdate\fP, char** \fIend\fP, time_t* \fIclock\fP)\fP
Parses the date specification
.I date
using the
\f[CB]tm_info.format\fP
string table (described below)
and returns the equivalent
\f[CB]time_t\fP
value.
If
\f[CB]non-NULL\fP,
.I end
is set to the position of the first unrecognized character in
.IR date .
.I clock
is used to provide default values for omitted components in
.IR date .
If
.I clock
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
then the current time is used.
.TP
\f[CB]int tmequiv(Tm_t* \fItp\fP)\fP
Returns the
\f[CB]time_t\fP
equivalent of
.IR tp ,
or the equivalent fields of
.I tp
relative to the current time if
.I tp
contains relative components.
.TP
\f[CB]Tm_t* tmfix(Tm_t* \fItp\fP)\fP
Corrects any out of bounds fields in
.I tp
and returns
.I tp
as its value.
The corrections start with
\f[CB]tp->tm_sec\fP
and propagate down to
.IR tp->tm_year .
For example, if
\f[CB]tp->tm_sec\fP
were 61 then it would change to 1 and
\f[CB]tp->tm_min\fP
would be incremented by 1, and so on.
\f[CB]tp->tm_isdst\fP
is not changed -- call
\f[CB]tmtime()\fP
to determine its proper value after the
\f[CB]tmfix()\fP
adjustments.
.TP
\f[CB]char* tmfmt(char *\fIrestrict buf\fP, size_t \fIlen\fP, const char *\fIrestrict format\fP, time_t* \fIclock\fP)\fP
Formats the date pointed to by
.I clock
into the buffer
.I buf
with size
.I len
bytes according to the format specification
.IR format .
If
.I format
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
or empty then the string
\f[CB]tm_info.format[40]\fP
is used.
If
.I clock
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
then the current time is used.
A pointer to the end of
.I buf
(i.e., the terminating
\f[CB]'\e0'\fP)
is returned.
.RS
.PP
.I format
is in
.BR printf (3)
style, where
.BI % field
names a fixed size field, zero padded if necessary,
and
.I \ec
and
.I \ennn
sequences are as in C. Invalid
.BI % field
specifications and all other characters are copied
without change.
.I field
may be preceded by
.B %-
to turn off padding or
.B %_
to pad with space, otherwise numeric fields
are padded with
.B 0
and string fields are padded with space.
.I field
may also be preceded by
.B E
for alternate era representation or
.B O
for alternate digit representation (if supported by the current locale).
Finally, an integral
.I width
preceding
.I field
truncates the field to
.I width
characters.
String field values are taken from the
\f[CB]tm_info.format\fP
string table.
The
.I fields
are:
.TP
.PD 0
.B %
.B %
character.
.TP
.B a
Abbreviated weekday name.
.TP
.B A
Full weekday name.
.TP
.B b
Abbreviated month name.
.TP
.B B
Full month name.
.TP
.B c
.BR ctime (3)
style date without the trailing
.BR newline .
.TP
.B C
.BR date (1)
style date.
.TP
.B d
Day of month number.
.TP
.B D
Date as
.IR mm / dd / yy .
.TP
.B e
Blank padded day of month number.
.TP
.B E
Unpadded day of month number.
.TP
.B f
Date as
.IR %Y.%m.%d-%H:%M:%S .
.TP
.B F
ISO 8601:2000 standard date format; equivalent to
.IR %Y-%m-%d .
.TP
.B g
2-digit year of the ISO week number
.BR (%V) .
.TP
.B G
4-digit year of the ISO week number
.BR (%V) .
.TP
.B h
Abbreviated month name.
.TP
.B H
24-hour clock hour, zero-padded.
.TP
.B i
International
.BR date (1)
date that includes the time zone type name
(\f[CB]tm_info.format[107]\fP).
.TP
.B I
12-hour clock hour, zero-padded.
.TP
.B j
1-offset Julian date.
.TP
.B J
0-offset Julian date.
.TP
.B k
24-hour clock hour, blank-padded.
.TP
.B K
Language neutral, all numeric, no embedded space date
with larger to smaller time units from left to right,
suitable for sorting:
\f[CB]"%Y-%m-%d+%H:%M:%S"\fP.
.TP
.B l
12-hour clock hour, blank-padded.
.TP
.B L
Obsolete; use
.BR %Ql .
.TP
.B m
Month number.
.TP
.B M
Minutes.
.TP
.B n
.B newline
character.
.TP
.B N
Nanoseconds 000000000-999999999.
.TP
.B p
Meridian (e.g.,
.B AM
or
.BR PM ).
.TP
.B P
Lowercase meridian (e.g.,
.B am
or
.BR pm ).
.TP
.B q
The quarter of the year.
.TP
\fB%Q\fP\fI<delim>recent<delim>distant<delim>\fP
Recent dates are formatted with
.I recent
and distant dates are formatted with
.IR distant ,
where
.I <delim>
is any character not appearing in
.I recent
or
.IR distant .
.TP
.B r
12-hour time as
.IR hh : mm : ss
.IR meridian .
.TP
.B R
24-hour time as
.IR hh : mm .
.TP
.B s
Seconds since the epoch.
.BI . prec
preceding
.B s
appends
.I prec
nanosecond digits,
.B 9
if
.I prec
is omitted.
.TP
.B S
.I seconds.subseconds
since the epoch.
.TP
.B t
.B tab
character.
.TP
.B T
24-hour time as
.IR hh : mm : ss .
.TP
.B u
Weekday number with 1 for Monday, 7 for Sunday.
.TP
.B U
Week number with Sunday as the first day.
.TP
.B V
ISO week number (i18n is \fIfun\fP).
.TP
.B w
Weekday number with 0 for Sunday, 6 for Saturday.
.TP
.B W
Week number with Monday as the first day.
.TP
.B x
Local date style, using
\f[CB]tm_info.format[39]\fP,
that includes the month, day and year.
.TP
.B X
Local time style, using
\f[CB]tm_info.format[38]\fP,
that includes the hours and minutes.
.TP
.B y
2-digit year (you'll be sorry).
.TP
.B Y
4-digit year.
.TP
.B z
Time zone
.I SHHMM
west of UTC offset where
.I S
is
.B +
or
.BR - .
.TP
.B Z
Time zone name.
.TP
\fB%=\fP[\fB=\fP][\fB-\fP|\fB+\fP]\fIflag\fP
Set (default or +) or clear (-)
.I flag
in
\f[CB]tm_info.flags\fP
for the remainder of
.IR format ,
or for the remainder of the process if
.B ==
is specified.
.I flag
may be:
.RS
.TP
.B l
\f[CB](TM_LEAP)\fP
Enable leap second adjustments.
.TP
.B s
\f[CB](TM_SUBSECOND)\fP
Append nanosecond
.B .%N
to
.BR %S .
.TP
.B u
\f[CB](TM_UTC)\fP
UTC time zone.
.RE
.TP
.B #
Equivalent to
.BR %s .
.TP
\fB%?\fP\fIalternate\fP
The
.I alternate
format is used if a default format override has not been specified.
e.g.,
.BR ls (1)
uses
.BR %?%l .
Export
\f[CB]TM_OPTIONS="format='\fP\fIoverride\fP\f[CB]'"\fP
to override the default.
.PD
.RE
.TP
\f[CB]void tminit(Tm_zone_t* \fIzone\fP, time_t \fInow\fP, const char \fInewzone\fP)\fP
Implicitly called by the other
.I tm
library routines to initialize global data, including the
\f[CB]tm_info.format\fP
table and the
\f[CB]tm_info.flags\fP
global flags.
Global data should only be modified after an explicit call to
\f[CB]tminit\fP.
If
\f[CB]zone != 0\fP
then it specifies a time zone other than the local time zone.
.TP
\f[CB]tmset(\fP\fIzone\fP, \fInow\fP, \fInewzone\fP\f[CB])\fP
is a macro equivalent to
\f[CB]tminit(\fP\fIzone\fP, \fInow\fP, \fInewzone\fP\f[CB])\fP;
it sets the reference timezone to
.IR zone .
\f[CB]tm_info.local\fP
points to the local timezone and
\f[CB]tm_info.zone\fP
points to the reference timezone at a given time
.I now
if
.I newzone
is non-zero, or to the current reference timezone otherwise.
.TP
\f[CB]time_t tmleap(time_t* \fIclock\fP)\fP
Returns a
\f[CB]time_t\fP
value for the time pointed to by
.I clock
with leap seconds adjusted for external
routines that do not handle leap seconds.
If
.I clock
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
then the current time is used.
Adjustments are only done if the
\f[CB]TM_ADJUST\fP
flag is set in
\f[CB]tm_info.flags\fP.
.TP
\f[CB]Tm_t* tmmake(time_t* \fIclock\fP)\fP
Returns a pointer to the
\f[CB]Tm_t\fP
struct corresponding to the time pointed to by
.IR clock .
If
.I clock
is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
then the current time is used.
.TP
\f[CB]int tmsleep(time_t \fIsec\fP, uint32_t \fInsec\fP)\fP
Suspends execution for
.I sec
seconds and
.I nsec
nanoseconds.
.TP
\f[CB]time_t tmtime(Tm_t* \fItp\fP, int \fIwest\fP)\fP
Returns the
\f[CB]time_t\fP
value corresponding to
.IR tp .
If
.I west
is
\f[CB]TM_LOCALZONE\fP
then
.I tm
is relative to the local time zone,
otherwise
.I west
is the number of minutes west of
.B UTC
with daylight savings time taken into account.
\f[CB]tp->tm_wday\fP,
\f[CB]tp->tm_yday\fP
and
\f[CB]tp->tm_isdst\fP
are ignored in the conversion.
.PP
The library routines use a table of date strings pointed to by
\f[CB]char** tm_info.format\fP.
The indices in
\f[CB]tm_info.format\fP
are fixed by category.
\f[CB]tm_info.format\fP
may be changed to point to other tables
according to local language and date conventions.
The contents by index (showing the USA English values) are:
.RS
.TP
.PD 0
.B 0-11
3-character abbreviated month names.
.TP
.B 12-23
Full month names.
.TP
.B 24-30
3-character abbreviated weekday names.
.TP
.B 31-37
Full weekday names.
.TP
.B 38
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
local time format used by the
.B %X
field.
.TP
.B 39
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
local date format used by the
.B %x
field.
.TP
.B 40
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
format used if the
.I format
argument is
\f[CB]NULL\fP
or empty.
.TP
.B 41-42
Meridian names: AM, PM.
.TP
.B 43-46
.B UTC
time zone names: UTC, UCT, CUT, GMT.
.TP
.B 47-50
Daylight savings time suffix names: DST.
.TP
.B 51-54
Suffixes to be ignored when matching strings in
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP.
.TP
.B 55-61
Time part names: second, hour, minute, day, week, month, year.
.TP
.B 62-65
Hours of the day names: midnight, morning, noon, evening.
.TP
.B 66-68
Relative day names: yesterday, today, tomorrow.
.TP
.B 69-71
Past relative time references: last, ago, past.
.TP
.B 72-74
Current relative time references: this, now, current.
.TP
.B 75-77
Future relative time references: next, hence, coming.
.TP
.B 78-80
Exact relative time references: exactly.
.TP
.B 81-84
Noise words to be ignored: at, in, on.
.TP
.B 85-94
Ordinal suffixes: st, nd, rd, th, th, th, th, th, th, th.
.TP
.B 95-104
Digit names.
.TP
.B 105
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
format equivalent for
.BR ctime (3):
\f[CB]"%a %b %e %T %Y"\fP.
.TP
.B 106
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
.BR date (1)
default format:
\f[CB]"%a %b %e %T %Z %Y"\fP.
.TP
.B 107
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
.BR date (1)
international format:
\f[CB]"%a %b %e %T %z %Z %Y"\fP.
.TP
.B 108
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
.BR ls (1)
recent date format:
\f[CB]"%b %e %H:%M"\fP.
.TP
.B 109
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
.BR ls (1)
distant date format:
\f[CB]"%b %e  %Y"\fP.
.TP
.B 110
The
\f[CB]tmfmt()\fP
.BR date (1)
meridian date format:
\f[CB]"%I:%M:%S %p"\fP.
.TP
.B 111
The ERA name.
.TP
.B 112
ERA alternative for
.BR 39 .
.TP
.B 113
ERA alternative for
.BR 38 .
.TP
.B 114
ERA alternative for
.BR 40 .
.TP
.B 115
The ERA year.
.TP
.B 116-125
Ordinal names: first, \fIno second!\fP, third, fourth, fifth, sixth, seventh,
eighth, ninth, tenth.
.TP
.B 126-128
Final time references, as in \fIthe last in the list\fP: final, ending, nth.
.TP
.B 129-131
Work time references: work, working, workday.
.PD
.RE
.PP
Low level support functions and data are described in
\f[CB]<tm.h>\fP.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.ft CR
#include <tm.h>
int main(void) {
    int       i;
    time_t    t;
    char      buf[128];
    struct {
        char* date;
        char* format;
    }         x[] = {
        "now",                 "%i",
        "2 months ago",        "%C",
        "this Wednesday noon", "%x %I:%M %p",
        "last December 25",    "%A",
        0,                     0
    };
    for (i = 0; x[i].date; i++) {
        t = tmdate(x[i].date, NULL, NULL);
        (void)tmfmt(buf, sizeof(buf), x[i].format, &t);
        puts(buf);
    }
}
.ft
.fi
produces
.nf
.ft CR
Fri Sep 30 12:10:14 USA EDT 1988
Fri Jul  1 00:00:00 EDT 1988
10/05/88 12:00 PM
Friday
.ft
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR date (1),
.IR time (2),
.IR ctime (3),
.IR tmx (3)
.SH BUGS
The C library static
\f[CB]struct tm\fP
values may get clobbered by
.I tm
library routines as the
.BR ctime (3)
and
.BR localtime (3)
routines typically return pointers to a single static
\f[CB]struct tm\fP
area.
\f[CB]tmdate()\fP
uses an internal international time zone name table that will
probably always be incomplete.
